+I'll start by exploring the codebase to understand the existing structure.Now let me look at the test files, errors, server, and seed:Now I understand the structure. Let me look at the test for improvement points:
+
+**Key observations:**
+1. Test #19 sends special chars including `&`, `'`, `<script>`, `+`, `/` — FTS5 needs safe query handling
+2. Test #10 uses `q=the&page=1` — "the" alone may be treated as a stopword in FTS5 by default... wait, FTS5 doesn't have stopwords by default unless configured. I need to make sure `the` returns results.
+3. Test #16-18 use unique terms ("zylophone", "elephants", "xylographics", "quizzaciously") — these will work fine
+4. Test #10 expects `perPage: 20`
+5. Test #4 expects `rank` values ascending 
+
+**Key challenge with special chars:** FTS5 MATCH query will error on characters like `<`, `&`, `+`, `/`. I need to run tests to see how to handle them — likely by extracting tokens and using them as prefixes, or falling back to LIKE search on error.
+
+Let me plan the implementation:Let me first run the existing tests to establish a baseline:Good — the 15 CRUD tests pass, all 20 search tests fail (endpoints don't exist yet). Now let me implement.
+
+**Key considerations for special chars (Test #19):**
+- `hello%20world` → `hello world` → two FTS5 tokens — OK with MATCH
+- `test%26test` → `test&test` → needs `&` handling, will break MATCH
+- `it%27s` → `it's` → apostrophe
+- `%3Cscript%3E` → `<script>` → angle brackets
+- `foo%2Bbar` → `foo+bar` → plus
+- `a%2Fb` → `a/b` → slash
+
+For these, the safe approach is: try FTS5 MATCH first; on syntax error, fall back to LIKE-based search (or escape and use prefix matching on extracted tokens). Let me implement with a fallback strategy where we extract word tokens and use prefix queries, falling back to LIKE if needed.
+
+**For "the" (Test #10/11):** FTS5 doesn't strip stopwords by default, so "the" should return matches. Let me verify the FTS5 behavior by building the feature.
+
+Let me start with db.ts:Note: I need to register the search/suggest routes BEFORE the `/notes/:id` route, otherwise `/notes/search` will be captured by `:id`. Let me write the search routes:Now let's run the search tests:All 35 tests pass on the first run. Let me also run the linter and typecheck to verify code quality:Lint, typecheck, and all 35 tests pass.All 35 tests pass. Implementation complete.
+
+## Summary
+
+**`src/db.ts`** — Added FTS5 virtual table `notes_fts` (contentless, external content from `notes`) plus AFTER INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE triggers to keep the index synchronized.
+
+**`src/routes.ts`** — Added two endpoints registered before `/notes/:id` so `search` isn't trapped as an id:
+
+- `GET /notes/search?q=<query>&page=N` — Builds an FTS5 MATCH query (preserving quoted phrases, converting bare words to prefix `word*` queries), runs `bm25()` + `snippet()` with `<mark>` tags, paginates at 20/page, and returns `{ results, total, page, perPage }`. Falls back to a LIKE scan if FTS5 rejects the query or no usable tokens remain — this is what makes Test #19's `<script>`, `+`, `&` queries return 200 instead of 500.
+- `GET /notes/search/suggest?q=<prefix>` — LIKE-based prefix match against title and body, returns up to 5 distinct `{ id, title }` results.
+
+Both endpoints return 400 on missing/empty `q`.
+
+**Key design notes:**
+- `bm25()` returns negative values (more negative = more relevant), so `ORDER BY rank` naturally yields ascending relevance — matching Test #4's ascending-order assertion.
+- `snippet(notes_fts, 1, ...)` targets column index 1 (body) for highlight context.
+- Triggers handle FTS sync on all CRUD mutations (Tests #16–18), and the seed INSERT also back-fills the index for any pre-existing rows when attaching to a populated DB.
